.TH "libmap" "7" "July 2026" "BetterScripts libmap v1.0.0" "Associative array emulation for POSIX.1 shells."
.hy
.SS \f[V]libmap.sh\f[R]
.SS SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[I]Full synopsis, description, arguments, examples and other
information is\f[R] \f[I]documented with each individual command.\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]map_new <MAP> --key|-k <KEY> --value|-v <VALUE>...\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]map_add <MAP> --key|-k <KEY> --value|-v <VALUE>...\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]map_set <MAP> --key|-k <KEY> --value|-v <VALUE>...\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]map_get <MAP> <KEY> [<OUTPUT>]\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]map_contains <MAP> <KEY>\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]map_delete <MAP> <KEY>...\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]map_size <MAP> [<OUTPUT>]\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]map_foreach <MAP> <COMMAND> [<ARGUMENT>...]\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]map_keys <MAP> [<OUTPUT>]\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]map_values <MAP> [<OUTPUT>]\f[R]
.PP
\f[V]map_is_map <MAP>\f[R]
.SS DESCRIPTION
.PP
Provides commands to allow any \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] compliant shell to use
emulated associative arrays (aka a \f[I]map\f[R], \f[I]hash\f[R],
\f[I]dictionary\f[R], or \f[I]symbol table\f[R]).
.SS EXIT STATUS
.IP \[bu] 2
For all commands the exit status will be \f[V]0\f[R] (\f[V]<zero>\f[R])
if, and only if, the command was completed successfully.
.IP \[bu] 2
For any command which is intended to perform a test, an exit status of
\f[V]1\f[R] (\f[V]<one>\f[R]) indicates \[lq]false\[rq], while
\f[V]0\f[R] (\f[V]<zero>\f[R]) indicates \[lq]true\[rq].
.IP \[bu] 2
An exit status that is \f[I]NOT\f[R] \f[V]0\f[R] (\f[V]<zero>\f[R]) from
an external command will be propagated to the caller where relevant (and
possible).
.IP \[bu] 2
Exit status\[cq] not covered by any of the above use values as described
in FreeBSD
\f[V]SYSEXITS(3)\f[R] (https://www.freebsd.org/cgi/man.cgi?sysexits(3))
- including the \f[V]EX_USAGE\f[R] which is used for all usage errors.
.IP \[bu] 2
Exit status is configuration agnostic.
.SS ENVIRONMENT
.PP
A number of environment variables affect the library, these are split
into variables that instruct the library to work-around specific
platform issues, and variables that convey user preferences.
Variables that enable platform specific work-arounds will be
automatically set if needed, but can also be set manually to force
specific configurations.
.PP
In additional to these, there are a number of variables that are set by
the library to convey information outside of command invocation.
.PP
If unset, some variables will take an initial value from a common
\f[V]shtoolkit\f[R] variable applicable to all libraries, these allow
the same configuration to be used across libraries more easily.
.PP
After the library has been sourced, external commands must not set
library environment variables that are classified as \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R].
Variables may use the \f[V]readonly\f[R] command to enforce this.
.PP
\f[B]\f[BI]If not otherwise specified, an \f[VBI]<unset>\f[BI] variable
is equivalent to the\f[B]\f[R] \f[B]\f[BI]default value.\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
\f[I]For more details see the \f[VI]shtoolkit\f[I] general
documentation.\f[R]
.SS PLATFORM CONFIGURATION
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBMAP_CONFIG_NO_Z_SHELL_SETOPT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Suite: \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_NO_Z_SHELL_SETOPT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]CONSTANT\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: <automatic>
.IP \[bu] 2
[Disable]/Enable using \f[V]setopt\f[R] in \f[I]Z Shell\f[R] to ensure
\f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] like behavior.
.IP \[bu] 2
Automatically enabled if \f[I]Z Shell\f[R] is detected.
.IP \[bu] 2
Any use of \f[V]setopt\f[R] is scoped as tightly as possible and SHOULD
not affect other commands.
.SS INFORMATIONAL
.PP
Variables that convey library information.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBMAP_VERSION_MAJOR\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Integer >= 1.
.IP \[bu] 2
Incremented when there are significant changes, or any changes break
compatibility with previous versions.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBMAP_VERSION_MINOR\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Integer >= 0.
.IP \[bu] 2
Incremented for significant changes that do not break compatibility with
previous versions.
.IP \[bu] 2
Reset to 0 when \f[V]BS_LIBMAP_VERSION_MAJOR\f[R] changes.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBMAP_VERSION_PATCH\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Integer >= 0.
.IP \[bu] 2
Incremented for minor revisions or bugfixes.
.IP \[bu] 2
Reset to 0 when \f[V]BS_LIBMAP_VERSION_MINOR\f[R] changes.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBMAP_VERSION_RELEASE\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
A string indicating a pre-release version, always null for full-release
versions.
.IP \[bu] 2
Possible values include `alpha', `beta', `rc', etc, (a numerical suffix
may also be appended).
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBMAP_VERSION_FULL\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Full version combining \f[V]BS_LIBMAP_VERSION_MAJOR\f[R],
\f[V]BS_LIBMAP_VERSION_MINOR\f[R], and \f[V]BS_LIBMAP_VERSION_PATCH\f[R]
as a single integer.
.IP \[bu] 2
Can be used in numerical comparisons
.IP \[bu] 2
Format: \f[V]MNNNPPP\f[R] where, \f[V]M\f[R] is the \f[V]MAJOR\f[R]
version, \f[V]NNN\f[R] is the \f[V]MINOR\f[R] version (3 digit, zero
padded), and \f[V]PPP\f[R] is the \f[V]PATCH\f[R] version (3 digit, zero
padded).
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBMAP_VERSION\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Full version combining \f[V]BS_LIBMAP_VERSION_MAJOR\f[R],
\f[V]BS_LIBMAP_VERSION_MINOR\f[R], \f[V]BS_LIBMAP_VERSION_PATCH\f[R],
and \f[V]BS_LIBMAP_VERSION_RELEASE\f[R] as a formatted string.
.IP \[bu] 2
Format:
\f[V]BetterScripts \[aq]libmap\[aq] vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH[-RELEASE]\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Derived tools MUST include unique identifying information in this value
that differentiates them from the BetterScripts versions.
(This information should precede the version number.)
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBMAP_MAP_FORMAT_VERSION\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Version of the internal data format for a map.
.IP \[bu] 2
Integer >= 1.
.IP \[bu] 2
Incremented when there are significant changes, or any changes break
compatibility with previous versions.
.IP \[bu] 2
Only a map with this version is supported for any command.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBMAP_LAST_ERROR\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Stores the error message of the most recent error.
.IP \[bu] 2
ONLY valid immediately following a command for which the exit status is
not \f[V]0\f[R] (\f[V]<zero>\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
Available even when error output is suppressed.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBMAP_SOURCED\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Set (and non-null) once the library has been sourced.
.IP \[bu] 2
Dependant scripts can query if this variable is set to determine if this
file has been sourced.
.SS USER PREFERENCE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBMAP_CONFIG_QUIET_ERRORS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Suite: \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_QUIET_ERRORS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]VARIABLE\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: \f[I]OFF\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
[Enable]/Disable library error message output.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: error messages will be written to \f[V]STDERR\f[R] as:
\f[V][libmap::<COMMAND>]: ERROR: <MESSAGE>\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: library error messages will be suppressed.
.IP \[bu] 2
The most recent error message is always available in
\f[V]BS_LIBMAP_LAST_ERROR\f[R] even when error output is suppressed.
.IP \[bu] 2
Both the library version of this option and the suite version can be
modified between command invocations and will affect the next command.
.IP \[bu] 2
Does NOT affect errors from non-library commands, which \f[I]may\f[R]
still produce output.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]BS_LIBMAP_CONFIG_FATAL_ERRORS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Suite: \f[V]BETTER_SCRIPTS_CONFIG_FATAL_ERRORS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Type: \f[I]FLAG\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Class: \f[I]VARIABLE\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Default: \f[I]OFF\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Enable/[Disable] causing library errors to terminate the current
(sub-)shell.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]OFF\f[R]: errors stop any further processing, and cause a non-zero
exit status, but do not cause an exception.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]ON\f[R]: any library error will cause an \[lq]unset variable\[rq]
shell exception using the
\f[V]${parameter:?[word]}\f[R] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition/utilities/V3_chap02.html#tag_18_06_02)
parameter expansion, where \f[V]word\f[R] is set to an error message
that \f[I]should\f[R] be displayed by the shell (this message is NOT
suppressed by \f[V]BS_LIBMAP_CONFIG_QUIET_ERRORS\f[R]).
.IP \[bu] 2
Both the library version of this option and the suite version can be
modified between command invocations and will affect the next command.
.SS COMMANDS
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]map_new\f[R]
.PP
Create a new map from the given arguments \f[I]or\f[R] a map written to
\f[V]STDOUT\f[R] with values from \f[V]STDIN\f[R].
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
\&... | map_new

map_new <MAP> --key|-k <KEY> --value|-v <VALUE>...

map_new <MAP> <KEY> <VALUE>...

map_new <MAP> <KEY>=<VALUE>...
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]MAP\f[R] [out:ref]
Variable that will contain the new map.
Any current contents will be lost.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name.
If specified as \f[V]-\f[R] (\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R]) map is written to
\f[V]STDOUT\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]KEY\f[R] [in]
Can contain any arbitrary text excluding any embedded \f[V]\[rs]0\f[R]
(\f[V]<NUL>\f[R]) characters.
MUST be followed by a \f[V]VALUE\f[R].
MUST NOT be null.
Can be specified multiple times.
.TP
\f[V]VALUE\f[R] [in]
Can contain any arbitrary text excluding any embedded \f[V]\[rs]0\f[R]
(\f[V]<NUL>\f[R]) characters.
MUST be preceded by a \f[V]KEY\f[R].
Can be null.
Can be specified multiple times.
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Map=$(my_command | map_new)
map_new \[aq]Map\[aq] \[aq]Key1=Value One\[aq] \[aq]Key2=Value Two\[aq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]CAVEATS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Argument forms can be mixed, but a \f[V]KEY\f[R] and a \f[V]VALUE\f[R]
MUST be paired.
.IP \[bu] 2
The use of \f[V]--key|-k\f[R] is \f[I]required\f[R] if \f[V]KEY\f[R]
contains an \f[V]=\f[R] (\f[V]<equals>\f[R]) character, otherwise all
forms are equivalent.
.IP \[bu] 2
When given no arguments, will read map values from \f[V]STDIN\f[R]; if
this is erroneously used without \f[V]STDIN\f[R] directed into the
command this will block indefinitely.
.IP \[bu] 2
Input via \f[V]STDIN\f[R] requires a single entry per line in the form
\f[V]<KEY>=<VALUE>\f[R].
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
An \f[V]ENTRY\f[R] specified as
\f[V]\[dq]\[aq]Key1\[aq]=\[aq]Value One\[aq]\[dq]\f[R] will have quotes
included in the values stored, i.e.\ \f[V]KEY\f[R] will be
\f[V]\[aq]Key1\[aq]\f[R] while \f[V]VALUE\f[R] will be
\f[V]\[aq]Value One\[aq]\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
Although it is possible to store any value in a map, large values (e.g.
file contents) may cause issues with performance or may be impossible
due to limitations of the current environment.
.IP \[bu] 2
When used with arguments, is identical to \f[V]map_add\f[R], but
discards any existing values in \f[V]MAP\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]map_new\f[R], \f[V]map_add\f[R], and \f[V]map_set\f[R] can all be
used interchangeably in some circumstances.
Where performance is concerned \f[V]map_new > map_add > map_set\f[R],
but the difference is unlikely to be of concern for most use cases (and
may very well not even be measurable).
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]map_add\f[R]
.PP
Add one or more values to a map.
Equivalent to \f[V]map_new\f[R] \f[I]except\f[R] any existing map values
are retained.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
map_add <MAP> --key|-k <KEY> --value|-v <VALUE>...

map_add <MAP> <KEY> <VALUE>...

map_add <MAP> <KEY>=<VALUE>...
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.PP
As for \f[V]map_new\f[R], with the exception that values can NOT be
specified via \f[V]STDIN\f[R], \f[I]and\f[R] any existing map values are
retained.
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Map=$(map_add - \[aq]A key for a map\[aq] \[aq]A value associated with the key\[aq])
map_add \[aq]Map\[aq] --key=\[dq]$FilePath\[dq] -v \[dq]$(cat $FilePath)\[dq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]CAVEATS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Using this command for entries that already exist will hide the existing
entries such that they can never be retrieved (or removed!)
from the map.
.IP \[bu] 2
As for \f[V]map_new\f[R].
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
As for \f[V]map_new\f[R].
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]map_set\f[R]
.PP
Update the value associated with an existing map entry, adding it if it
does not exist.
.PP
If the entry does not exist, this is equivalent to \f[V]map_add\f[R].
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
map_set <MAP> --key|-k <KEY> --value|-v <VALUE>...

map_set <MAP> <KEY> <VALUE>...

map_set <MAP> <KEY>=<VALUE>...
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.PP
As for \f[V]map_add\f[R], except \f[V]MAP\f[R] can not be \f[V]-\f[R]
(\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R]).
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Map=$(map_set - \[aq]A key for a map\[aq] \[aq]A value associated with the key\[aq])
map_set \[aq]Map\[aq] --key=\[dq]$FilePath\[dq] -v \[dq]$(cat $FilePath)\[dq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]CAVEATS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
As for \f[V]map_add\f[R].
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]MAP\f[R] can not be specified as \f[V]-\f[R] (\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R]),
i.e.\ input can not be taken from \f[V]STDIN\f[R], and output can not be
sent to \f[V]STDOUT\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
As for \f[V]map_add\f[R].
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]map_get\f[R]
.PP
Lookup a map value.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
map_get <MAP> <KEY> [<OUTPUT>]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]MAP\f[R] [in:ref]
Variable containing a map.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name.
.TP
\f[V]KEY\f[R] [in]
Can contain any arbitrary text excluding any embedded \f[V]\[rs]0\f[R]
(\f[V]<NUL>\f[R]) characters.
Must NOT be null.
.TP
\f[V]OUTPUT\f[R] [out:ref]
Variable that will contain the element value.
Any current contents will be lost.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name or a \f[V]-\f[R]
(\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R]).
If not specified, or specified as \f[V]-\f[R] (\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R]) value
is written to \f[V]STDOUT\f[R].
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
if map_get \[aq]Map\[aq] \[aq]A map key\[aq] \[aq]ValueVar\[aq]; then ... fi
ValueVar=$(map_get \[aq]Map\[aq] \[aq]A map key\[aq])
ValueVar=$(map_get \[aq]Map\[aq] \[aq]A map key\[aq] -)
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
On success the exit code will be \f[V]0\f[R] (\f[V]<zero>\f[R]) if the
\f[V]KEY\f[R] exists and \f[V]1\f[R] (\f[V]<one>\f[R]) if it does not.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]OUTPUT\f[R] is only modified if \f[V]KEY\f[R] is found.
.IP \[bu] 2
If value is output to \f[V]STDOUT\f[R] data \f[I]may\f[R] be lost if the
value ends with a \f[V]\[rs]n\f[R] (\f[V]<newline>\f[R])
(\f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] rules state that newlines should be removed from the
end of output generated by commands).
.IP \[bu] 2
If \f[V]OUTPUT\f[R] is not required, \f[V]map_contains\f[R] is faster
for checking if a \f[V]KEY\f[R] exists, though the difference is likely
to be relatively small.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]map_contains\f[R]
.PP
Check if a map contains a key.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
map_contains <MAP> <KEY>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]MAP\f[R] [in:ref]
Variable containing a map.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name.
.TP
\f[V]KEY\f[R] [in]
Can contain any arbitrary text excluding any embedded \f[V]\[rs]0\f[R]
(\f[V]<NUL>\f[R]) characters.
Must NOT be null.
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
if map_contains \[aq]Map\[aq] \[aq]A map key\[aq]; then ... fi
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]map_contains\f[R] is faster than \f[V]map_get\f[R] when testing if
\f[V]KEY\f[R] exists (when the value is not required), but the
difference is likely to be relatively small.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]map_delete\f[R]
.PP
Remove a map entry.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
map_delete <MAP> <KEY>...
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]MAP\f[R] [out:ref]
Variable containing a map.
Any current contents will be lost.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name.
.TP
\f[V]KEY\f[R] [in]
Can contain any arbitrary text excluding any embedded \f[V]\[rs]0\f[R]
(\f[V]<NUL>\f[R]) characters.
Must NOT be null.
Can be specified multiple times.
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
map_delete \[aq]Map\[aq] \[aq]A map key\[aq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Any \f[V]KEY\f[R] which is not in \f[V]MAP\f[R] is skipped.
This is not considered an error.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]map_size\f[R]
.PP
Get the number of entries in a map.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
map_size <MAP> [<OUTPUT>]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]MAP\f[R] [in:ref]
Variable containing a map.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name.
Can reference an empty map or \f[V]unset\f[R] variable.
.TP
\f[V]OUTPUT\f[R] [out:ref]
Variable that will contain the map size.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name.
Any current contents will be lost.
If not specified, or specified as \f[V]-\f[R] (\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R]) size
is written to \f[V]STDOUT\f[R].
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Size=$(map_size \[aq]Map\[aq])
Size=$(map_size \[aq]Map\[aq] -)
map_size \[aq]Map\[aq] \[aq]Size\[aq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]map_foreach\f[R]
.PP
Invoke a given command for each map entry.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
map_foreach <MAP> <COMMAND> [<ARGUMENTS>...]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]MAP\f[R] [in:ref]
Variable that contains a map.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name.
.TP
\f[V]COMMAND\f[R] [in]
Command to invoke for each map entry.
.TP
\f[V]ARGUMENTS\f[R] [in]
Arguments passed to \f[V]COMMAND\f[R].
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
map_foreach MAP printf \[aq]Key: %s; Value: %s\[rs]n\[aq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]CAVEATS\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]COMMAND\f[R] may invoke other map commands, but changes to
\f[V]MAP\f[R] will NOT affect \f[V]map_foreach\f[R] (e.g.\ an entry
added to \f[V]MAP\f[R] via \f[V]COMMAND\f[R] will NOT be iterated by
\f[V]map_foreach\f[R] - similarly for deleted or edited entries).
.IP \[bu] 2
If \f[V]COMMAND\f[R] invokes \f[V]map_foreach\f[R] it \f[B]MUST\f[R] be
inside a subshell local to \f[V]COMMAND\f[R].
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]COMMAND\f[R] is invoked with the the map key and value as the final
two arguments.
.IP \[bu] 2
Iteration is stopped if \f[V]COMMAND\f[R] results in a non-zero exit
code.
This code will be used as the exit code for \f[V]map_foreach\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
The order entries are iterated in is deterministic \f[I]but\f[R] may not
match the order they were added.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]map_keys\f[R]
.PP
Get an array of all keys in a map.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
map_keys <MAP> [<OUTPUT>]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]MAP\f[R] [in:ref]
Variable that contains a map.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name.
.TP
\f[V]OUTPUT\f[R] [out:ref]
Variable that will contain the keys.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name.
Any current contents will be lost.
If not specified, or specified as \f[V]-\f[R] (\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R]) the
keys are written to \f[V]STDOUT\f[R].
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
map_keys Map Keys
eval \[dq]set -- $Keys\[dq]
for Key; do print \[aq]%s\[rs]n\[aq] \[dq]$Key\[dq]; done
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
The order of values is deterministic but may not match the order they
were added.
.IP \[bu] 2
The resulting array can be manipulated using the \f[V]libarray.sh\f[R]
library (also part of the \f[V]shtoolkit\f[R]).
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]map_values\f[R]
.PP
Get an array of all values in a map.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
map_values <MAP> [<OUTPUT>]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]MAP\f[R] [in:ref]
Variable containing a map.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name.
.TP
\f[V]OUTPUT\f[R] [out:ref]
Variable that will contain the keys.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name.
Any current contents will be lost.
If not specified, or specified as \f[V]-\f[R] (\f[V]<hyphen>\f[R]) the
keys are written to \f[V]STDOUT\f[R].
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
map_values Map Values
eval \[dq]set -- $Values\[dq]
for Val; do print \[aq]%s\[rs]n\[aq] \[dq]$Val\[dq]; done
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
The order of values is deterministic but may not match the order they
were added.
.IP \[bu] 2
The resulting array can be manipulated using the \f[V]libarray.sh\f[R]
library (also part of the \f[V]shtoolkit\f[R]).
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS \f[V]map_is_map\f[R]
.PP
Determine if a variable looks like it contains map like data.
.PP
\f[I]SYNOPSIS\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
map_is_map <MAP>
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]ARGUMENTS\f[R]
.TP
\f[V]MAP\f[R] [in:ref]
Variable that may contain a map.
MUST be a valid \f[I]POSIX.1\f[R] name.
.PP
\f[I]EXAMPLES\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
if map_is_map \[aq]Var\[aq]; then ...; fi
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[I]NOTES\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
An empty or unset \f[V]MAP\f[R] is \f[I]not\f[R] a valid map.
.IP \[bu] 2
Exit status will be \f[V]0\f[R] (\f[V]<zero>\f[R]) if \f[V]MAP\f[R]
appears to be a valid map, while the exit status will be \f[V]1\f[R]
(\f[V]<one>\f[R]) in all other (non-error) cases.
.SS STANDARDS
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[I]POSIX.1-2008\f[R] (https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799.2008edition).
.IP \[bu] 2
FreeBSD SYSEXITS(3) (https://www.freebsd.org/cgi/man.cgi?sysexits(3)).
.IP \[bu] 2
Semantic Versioning v2.0.0 (https://semver.org/).
.IP \[bu] 2
Inclusive Naming Initiative (https://inclusivenaming.org/).
.PP
\f[I]For more details see the \f[VI]shtoolkit\f[I] general
documentation.\f[R]
.SS NOTES
.IP \[bu] 2
Modification of a map outside of the library is not supported.
.IP \[bu] 2
Argument validation occurs where possible and (relatively) performant
for all arguments to all commands.
.IP \[bu] 2
Maps can be serialized (e.g.\ saved to, or loaded from, a file).
The \f[V]BS_LIBMAP_MAP_FORMAT_VERSION\f[R] value determines if a library
is able to process a given map and \f[I]may\f[R] change between library
versions.
.SS CAVEATS
.IP \[bu] 2
The library attempts to account for differences between implementations
(where known), however, it is not possible to do this for every case.
.IP \[bu] 2
The maximum size of any map is limited by the environment in which it is
used.
Of particular note is that exceeding the command line length limit will
cause maps to be unusable in many (platform dependent) circumstances,
though other limitations will also exist.
Note that exporting a variable containing an map will cause that
variable to be counted against the command line length limit
\f[B]TWICE\f[R] if the map is also used with a command.
.IP \[bu] 2
The internal structure of a map is subject to change without notice and
should not be relied upon.
.PP
\f[I]For more details see the \f[VI]shtoolkit\f[I] general
documentation.\f[R]
.SS SEE ALSO
